[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-109390"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3.$ Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3\\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-110381/selection/d36ea25c11725150c248b2cb2527287b8b8704aa9a3d1b7bc459b01f5c69d84a" :answer/id #uuid "6f32e164-5a32-570c-8cf2-2d9d43f8f968" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110381/selection/23c6f06eea5f421c768715d850356dd2c2eb38743f936bf7e39e6ee8654fbaf3" :answer/id #uuid "edcbc31a-2d3f-5330-8997-a7710ff80ccb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110381/selection/2ce23049e7b6fd23163452a9e94594e97ef583ce9bfabf1eac26aeeb81ad6f77" :answer/id #uuid "0a850a69-1532-5822-8a1d-9ad0d01463fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110381/selection/6ce741962ebd1db16724fee373592381a580e1e0161473d71ca2b73453308b58" :answer/id #uuid "14dd41f2-cfd5-5849-8d67-ef1a0eacb9ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 